arXiv:2602.21496v1 [cs.AI] 25 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Umid Suleymanov
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Virginia Tech
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:umids@vt.edu">umids@vt.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zaur Rajabov
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of IT and Engineering, ADA University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Emil Mirzazada
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of IT and Engineering, ADA University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Murat Kantarcioglu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science, Virginia Tech
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">While defenses for structured PII are mature, Large Language Models (LLMs) pose a new threat: <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Sensitive Information</span> (SemSI), where models infer <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sensitive identity attributes</span>, generate <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reputation-harmful content</span>, or hallucinate <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">potentially wrong information</span>. The capacity of LLMs to self-regulate these complex, context-dependent sensitive information leaks without destroying utility remains an open scientific question. To address this, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>, an inference-time framework where an agentic ”Editor” iteratively critiques and <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rewrites</span> sensitive spans to preserve narrative flow rather than simply refusing to answer. Our analysis reveals a <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-Utility Pareto Frontier</span>, where this agentic rewriting reduces leakage by <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.6%</span> across all three SemSI categories while incurring a marginal utility loss (<span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.8%</span>). We also uncover a <span id="abstract1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale-Dependent Safety Divergence</span>: large reasoning models (e.g., GPT-5) achieve safety through <span id="abstract1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constructive expansion</span> (adding nuance), whereas capacity-constrained models revert to <span id="abstract1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">destructive truncation</span> (deleting text). Finally, we identify a <span id="abstract1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning Paradox</span>: while inference-time reasoning increases baseline risk by enabling the model to make deeper sensitive inferences, it simultaneously empowers the defense to execute safe rewrites.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, Large Language Models, Privacy, Agentic Systems, AI Safety, Reasoning, Inference-Time Intervention, Semantic Sensitive Information
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While traditional privacy defenses successfully mask <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Structured Sensitive Information</span> (PII) via pattern matching (e.g., regex for emails or SSNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">da Silva, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni and Cauvery, 2021</a>)</cite>, they fail against a more insidious threat: <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Sensitive Information</span> (<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSI</span>). Unlike PII, SemSI involves contextual leaks where sensitive inferences emerge from the narrative structure itself rather than specific keywords. As formalized by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, these leaks fall into three distinct categories: <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitive Identity Attributes</span>, defined as non-structured personal privacy-related information (e.g., religious beliefs, political opinions); <span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reputation-Harmful Content</span>, involving the generation of defamatory viewpoints that damage an individual’s standing; and <span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incorrect Hazardous Information</span>, where the model validates false premises that threaten public safety (e.g., confirming that ”disinfectants cure COVID-19”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the threat is known, the community lacks a fundamental understanding of the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">defense surface</span>. Unlike PII, mitigating SemSI requires the model to distinguish between benign facts and sensitive inferences, and then <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">restructure</span> the narrative to preserve utility. Table <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates this necessity: when asked why NYC uses ”unsafe” pesticides (a query containing <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Incorrect Hazardous Information</span>), a standard model often attempts to justify the false premise, inadvertently validating the hazard. In contrast, a semantically secure model must <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">restructure</span> the response, explicitly correcting the false premise (citing EPA safety standards) while still answering the underlying question about pesticide use. Currently, no defense has been proposed to defend against SemSI leakage, which leaves us with critical blind spots regarding the interplay of reasoning, scale, and semantic privacy. To address this gap, we structure our investigation around four core scientific questions:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can LLMs rely on their own reasoning capabilities to detect and correct semantic leakage in open-ended generation?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can agentic defenses mitigate privacy risks without imposing a severe ”Utility Loss” (i.e., destroying helpfulness via refusal)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How do model scale and inference-time reasoning affect safety behaviors? Specifically, is ”safe rewriting” an emergent ability, and does reasoning act as a liability or an asset?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is semantic leakage primarily a failure of privacy or a failure of truthfulness (hallucination), and can agentic defenses restore epistemic calibration?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To answer these questions, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>, a novel inference-time defense framework designed to probe the limits of LLM self-correction. Recognizing that semantic privacy requires reasoning rather than pattern matching, our approach employs a dual-agent architecture (Evaluator-Editor) that iteratively critiques and rewrites content. We utilize <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> not merely as a tool, but as a lens to test the privacy capabilities of 13 SOTA LLMs (including GPT-5) against the <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSI-Bench</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our primary contributions as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantifying the Privacy-Utility Frontier:</span> We provide the first empirical analysis of the ”Utility Loss” inherent in SemSI mitigation. We demonstrate that <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>’s agentic rewriting enables reducing semantic leakage by an average of <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.6%</span> while incurring only a marginal <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.8%</span> cost in utility.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unveiling Scale-Dependent Safety Behaviors:</span> We report a novel divergence in model behavior under safety pressure. Larger models (e.g., GPT-5) utilize feedback to <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">synthesize</span> safer narratives, often increasing answer length to explain nuance (preserving utility). In contrast, smaller models (e.g., Llama-3, Gemma) lack the reasoning capacity to rewrite effectively, resorting to aggressive truncation or refusal (destroying utility). This suggests that ”Safety via Rewriting” is an emergent ability of larger reasoning models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epistemic Calibration:</span> We identify that semantic leakage often stems from model overconfidence, where models hallucinate plausible but unverified details to satisfy a prompt. We demonstrate that <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> restores <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">epistemic calibration</span>, shifting models from confident hallucination to ”qualified responses” (explicit admissions of uncertainty). This eliminates the generation of false information (reducing the rate from 15.9% to 0% in GPT-5) and reveals a scaling law where larger models retain high utility by providing corrective context instead of simple refusals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The <span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> Framework:</span> To enable these insights, we propose an iterative, inference-time agentic algorithm. <span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> dynamically identifies and restructures specific narrative segments containing sensitive inferences to maintain text fluency, establishing a new state-of-the-art for semantic defenses.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21496v1/example.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/643;" width="429" height="643" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mitigation of Incorrect Hazardous Information.</span> An example of <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> handling the ”Incorrect Hazardous Information” category of SemSI as defined by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21496v1/method_figure.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Schematic overview of the <span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The landscape of privacy preservation in Large Language Models (LLMs) has shifted from the static masking of structured data to the dynamic mitigation of semantic risks. Our work addresses the intersection of three active research streams: (1) the transition from Structured PII to Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI), (2) the utility costs of inference-time defenses, and (3) the scaling limits of agentic self-correction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From Structured PII to Inferential Privacy</span>.
Historically, privacy defenses in NLP focused on <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured Sensitive Information</span>, primarily Personally Identifiable Information (PII) such as phone numbers or email addresses. Traditional approaches rely on pattern matching via regular expressions or Named Entity Recognition (NER) models to detect and mask these tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">da Silva, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kulkarni and Cauvery, 2021</a>)</cite>. Tools like Microsoft Presidio and Google Cloud DLP exemplify this “match-and-mask” paradigm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, recent study highlights the inadequacy of pattern-based defenses against <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inferential Privacy</span> risks. Staab et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Staab et al., 2024</a>)</cite> demonstrated that LLMs can accurately infer sensitive attributes (e.g., location, income, political affiliation) from seemingly benign text, achieving high accuracy even when all structured PII is redacted. This confirms that privacy leakage is often contextual rather than explicit. Building on this, Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> formalized <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI)</span>—content defined by sensitive <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">meaning</span> (e.g., reputation-harming inferences or hazardous information) rather than syntax. Unlike PII, SemSI cannot be captured by static lexicons and is often entangled with the model’s tendency to hallucinate convincing but defamatory details
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, necessitating defenses that possess semantic reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-Time Defenses and the Utility Trade-off</span>
To address risks during generation, the field has adopted <span id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-Time Defenses</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The dominant industrial standard involves “Guardrails”—external safety classifiers like <span id="S2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama Guard 4</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2025</a>)</cite> or NeMo Guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rebedea et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which screen inputs and outputs for violations. While effective at blocking harm, these systems operate on a <span id="S2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refusal Basis</span>: when a violation is detected, they trigger a binary refusal (e.g., “I cannot answer this”), destroying the utility of the response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Alternative approaches explore <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-Conscious Delegation</span>. For instance, <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrivacyPAD</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hui et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses Reinforcement Learning to route queries between a secure local model and a powerful remote model, masking sensitive chunks before transmission. However, masking often disrupts the narrative flow. Other methods, such as <span id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrivRewrite</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim, 2025</a>)</cite>, utilize Differential Privacy (DP) mechanisms to select rewrites. While offering formal guarantees, DP-based noise frequently results in linguistic degradation, rendering the text disjointed. A key open question remains: <span id="S2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is it possible to mitigate semantic privacy risks without incurring this severe utility loss?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic Feedback and Self-Correction</span>
Our approach leverages the emerging paradigm of <span id="S2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic Self-Correction</span>, where LLMs iteratively critique and improve their own outputs. Frameworks like <span id="S2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Self-Refine</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <span id="S2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Critic</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> demonstrated that iterative feedback loops significantly improve performance in reasoning and code generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">While recent works have applied recursive oversight to safety—such as Constitutional AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite> for alignment or recursive checks against jailbreaks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robey et al., 2023</a>)</cite>—two critical gaps remain. First, existing methods typically focus on compliance (blocking toxic content) rather than the intricate editorial work required to rewrite a narrative that is safe but remains informative. Second, the impact of <span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Scale</span> on self-correction capabilities is under-explored in the privacy domain. It remains unclear whether smaller models possess the reasoning capacity to “edit” rather than “refuse.” Our <span id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> framework addresses these gaps by<span id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">employing an Evaluator-Editor loop to probe the privacy-utility frontier and analyze scale-dependent safety behaviors.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> Inference Loop</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_figure_panel ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Query <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, Model <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, Editor Model <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{ed}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{ed}</annotation></semantics></math>, Evaluator Model <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{ev}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{ev}</annotation></semantics></math>, Prompts <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{\mathrm{init}},p_{\mathrm{eval}},p_{\mathrm{edit}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>init</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>edit</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{\mathrm{init}},p_{\mathrm{eval}},p_{\mathrm{edit}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, Budget <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Sanitized Response <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{final}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{final}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
           {<span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Phase 1: Initialization</span>}

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{0}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(p_{\mathrm{init}}\|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>init</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(p_{\mathrm{init}}\|x)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
           {<span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Phase 2: Refinement Loop</span>}

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
