           <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> iteration <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{ev}(p_{\mathrm{eval}}\|x\|y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{ev}(p_{\mathrm{eval}}\|x\|y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generate Critique</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Stop</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">break</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{ed}(p_{\mathrm{edit}}\|y_{t}\|f_{e_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>edit</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{ed}(p_{\mathrm{edit}}\|y_{t}\|f_{e_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Apply Edits</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1px;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="alg1.7" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="alg1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Stop Condition <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Stop</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)</annotation></semantics></math>:<span id="alg1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Returns <span id="alg1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">True</span> if:</span></span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_figure_panel ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">SemSI</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> detection fields in </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> contain all negative responses (</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{"ifPrivacy": "no", "ifHarmful": "no", "ifMisinformation": "no"}</span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">), indicating a clean response, </span><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">OR</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (Computational budget exhausted).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> Framework</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>, an inference-time defense mechanism designed to iteratively detect and mitigate Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI) without retraining the underlying model (See Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for an overview). The framework operates as a dual-agent loop, comprising an <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluator</span> and an <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Editor</span>, that mimics human editorial oversight. Formally, given a frozen Large Language Model <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and an input query <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the process unfolds in two phases: <span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Initialization</span> and <span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iterative Refinement</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Initialization.</span>

The process begins with the generation of a baseline response candidate. Unlike standard generation, we wrap the input query <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> within a task-specific system prompt <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>init</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{init}}</annotation></semantics></math> designed to encourage safety-conscious drafting. The model <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> generates the initial draft <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{0}\;=\;\mathcal{M}(p_{\mathrm{init}}\;\|\;x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>init</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}\;=\;\mathcal{M}(p_{\mathrm{init}}\;\|\;x),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes string concatenation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: The Evaluator-Editor Loop.</span>
Following initialization, the algorithm enters a feedback loop indexed by <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
This phase employs two distinct agents: an Editor Agent, denoted by <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{ed}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{ed}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an Evaluator Agent, denoted by <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{ev}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{ev}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Evaluator Agent (Critique).</span>
The Evaluator assesses the current draft <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for semantic leakage. Crucially, the Evaluator is conditioned on both the original query <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (to maintain context) and the current draft <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ few-shot prompting in <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{eval}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{eval}}</annotation></semantics></math> containing pairs <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{i},y_{i}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{i},y_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to ground the model’s judgment. The Evaluator produces structured feedback <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}\;=\;\mathcal{M}_{ev}(p_{\mathrm{eval}}\;\|\;x\;\|\;y_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>eval</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}\;=\;\mathcal{M}_{ev}(p_{\mathrm{eval}}\;\|\;x\;\|\;y_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math>
The feedback <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains fields indicating the presence of SemSI (e.g., <span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{"ifHarmful": "yes", "harmful": "...", ...}</span>) and specific reasoning traces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Editor Agent (Refinement).</span>
If SemSI is detected, the Editor Agent <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{ed}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{ed}</annotation></semantics></math> receives the current draft <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Evaluator’s critique <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its objective is to generate a revised draft <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> that preserves the utility of <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> while resolving the specific privacy violations identified in <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> by generating
<math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}\;=\;\mathcal{M}_{ed}(p_{\mathrm{edit}}\;\|\;y_{t}\;\|\;f_{e_{t}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>edit</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}\;=\;\mathcal{M}_{ed}(p_{\mathrm{edit}}\;\|\;y_{t}\;\|\;f_{e_{t}}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3: Convergence &amp; Stop Condition.</span>
The loop terminates when a stop function <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Stop</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Stop}(f_{e_{t}},t)</annotation></semantics></math> returns <span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">True</span>. This occurs under two conditions: (1) <span id="S3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence</span>: The Evaluator detects no semantic leakage in <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{e_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{e_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted by a clean evaluation), or (2) <span id="S3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Budget Constraint</span>: The iteration count <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> reaches a pre-defined maximum <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final output <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\mathrm{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>final</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\mathrm{final}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at termination. The full procedure is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks and Dataset</span>.
To benchmark semantic leakage, we utilize the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSI-Bench</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which consists of curated questions designed to elicit sensitive information across diverse domains. These questions were sourced from fact-checking repositories and include statements of varying truthfulness, making them ideal for testing the boundaries of model knowledge and hallucination.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For our defense instruction set, we utilized synthetically generated and manually refined prompts (detailed in Appendix A). These instructions incorporate advanced prompt engineering techniques, including few-shot prompting and ”sandwich” instructions, to maximize adherence during the <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> loop.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines and Control</span>.
To rigorously evaluate the contribution of our agentic feedback loop, we compare <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> against three distinct control settings. First, we define the <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Protection</span> setting as the raw, zero-shot behavior of the model, following the generation protocol of <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSI-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In this approach, no safety instructions are provided; the model is prompted solely with the benchmark question in a single interaction to establish the intrinsic leakage rate. Second, to isolate the impact of our iterative feedback mechanism, we establish a <span id="S4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Baseline</span> (Prompt-Only) approach. This method utilizes the same safety-oriented system prompt (Preprocessing Instructions) as our proposed method but operates in a single pass without any agentic self-correction loop. Finally, we evaluate the mitigation efficacy of state-of-the-art external guardrails, specifically <span id="S4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LlamaGuard 4</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2025</a>)</cite> and <span id="S4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-OSS-Safeguard-20B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>. These models are applied as output filters to measure their ability to detect and block Semantic Sensitive Information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Results on <span id="S4.T1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSI-Bench</span>.</span> We compare <span id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> against two control settings: <span id="S4.T1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Protection</span> (standard generation) and <span id="S4.T1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline</span> (static prompt-based defense) across 13 SOTA models. <span id="S4.T1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> indicates the best performance (lowest for privacy/toxicity, highest for utility). Additionally, we evaluate two external guardrails, <span id="S4.T1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LlamaGuard 4</span> (LG4) and <span id="S4.T1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-OSS-Safeguard-20B</span>, reporting their F1 scores in detecting SemSI. The near-zero detection scores (avg. 0.01 and 0.17) confirm that current safety classifiers fail to recognize semantic leakage, whereas <span id="S4.T1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> significantly reduces leakage occurrence while maintaining high utility.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.18" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:85.9pt;vertical-align:-41.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-241.4pt,60.1pt) scale(0.416752653310038,0.416752653310038) ;">
