<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Methodology</span>.
We propose a multi-dimensional evaluation framework that assesses the trade-off between privacy preservation and response quality. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first to formalize and quantify the <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy-Utility Trade-off</span> specifically for the task of Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI) mitigation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy Evaluation (Revised SemSI Judges)</span>.
We quantify semantic leakage using LLM-as-a-Judge agents. While Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposed initial evaluation prompts, we observed that they were often too vague, leading to false positives where models flagged benign paraphrases of the question itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we introduce <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refined SemSI Judges</span> (prompts in Appendix  <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Prompts for SemSI Types ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). These judges utilize stricter definitions for three leakage categories: <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sensitive Identity Attributes</span>, <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reputation-Harmful Content</span>, and <span id="S4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hazardous information</span>. The judges output a toxicity score <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{0,\dots,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{0,\dots,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where 0 indicates no leakage and 3 indicates severe harm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility Evaluation (Novel Metric)</span>. A defense mechanism that returns empty responses would achieve perfect privacy but zero utility. To prevent such trivial solutions, we explicitly measure the <span id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility Loss</span> introduced by our defense. We employ a separate LLM judge <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{judge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>judge</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{judge}}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate responses on three axes (0-10 scale): <span id="S4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relevance (<math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{rel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{rel}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>, regarding whether the response directly addresses the user’s intent; <span id="S4.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness (<math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{corr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>corr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{corr}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>, regarding whether the information is factually accurate and free of hallucinations; and <span id="S4.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completeness (<math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{comp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>comp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{comp}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>, regarding whether the response covers the necessary details without being overly redacted. Formally, for a given input <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and response <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, the utility score <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{util}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>util</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{util}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as the mean of these components: <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{\mathrm{rel}},s_{\mathrm{corr}},s_{\mathrm{comp}})\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{judge}}(p_{\mathrm{util}}\|x\|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>corr</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>comp</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>judge</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>util</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{\mathrm{rel}},s_{\mathrm{corr}},s_{\mathrm{comp}})\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{judge}}(p_{\mathrm{util}}\|x\|y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{util}}=\frac{1}{3}(s_{\mathrm{rel}}+s_{\mathrm{corr}}+s_{\mathrm{comp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>util</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>corr</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>comp</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{util}}=\frac{1}{3}(s_{\mathrm{rel}}+s_{\mathrm{corr}}+s_{\mathrm{comp}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation allows us to strictly penalize defenses that ”safe-guard” by refusing to answer benign queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Empirical Results &amp; Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We structure our analysis to answer the research questions posed in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, probing the capabilities, trade-offs, and behavioral divergences of LLMs under agentic safety pressure. We present a summary of our core discoveries below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>The Privacy-Utility Frontier</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary objective was to determine if <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> could mitigate semantic leakage without destroying response utility (RQ1 &amp; RQ2). Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the comparative performance across 13 SOTA models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy Gains.</span>
We observe a systematic reduction in SemSI across all families. On average, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> lowers leakage occurrence from <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.78</span> to <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.51</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.6%</span> reduction), corroborated by a toxicity drop from <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.85</span> to <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.45</span>. Notably, high-baseline models like GPT-OSS-20B (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.97</span>) see dramatic reductions (to <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.38</span>) under agentic oversight, proving the method reduces both frequency and severity of leakage. Crucially, these gains preserve utility. As shown in the Pareto analysis (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 The Privacy-Utility Frontier ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), average utility decreases marginally from <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.04</span> to <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.45</span> (a <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.8%</span> cost).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure of Static Guardrails.</span>
Existing safety classifiers fail to detect semantic leakage. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LlamaGuard 4</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-OSS-Safeguard-20B</span> achieve negligible F1 scores (<span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.01</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.17</span>), confirming they are ”blind” to context-dependent nuances. This underscores the insufficiency of post-hoc detection and validates the necessity of intrinsic, reasoning-based defenses like <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21496v1/privacy_utility.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S5.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Privacy-Utility Pareto Frontier.</span> A scatter plot visualizing the efficiency of <span id="S5.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>. The y-axis represents the <span id="S5.F3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Privacy Gain</span> (reduction in SemSI), while the x-axis represents the <span id="S5.F3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Utility Cost</span> (reduction in response quality). Ideally, methods should appear in the Top-Left quadrant (High Gain, Low Cost). The data reveals a favorable exchange rate: on average, models achieve a <span id="S5.F3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.6%</span> reduction in semantic leakage while sacrificing only <span id="S5.F3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.8%</span> in utility.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21496v1/iteration_stats.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence Efficiency by Model Scale.</span> Larger models (Left) frequently satisfy safety constraints during the initialization phase (Pink), requiring zero additional feedback loops. In contrast, smaller models (Right) often fail to converge, exhausting the maximum iteration budget (Blue) without resolving the semantic leakage.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21496v1/answer_length_decrease.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The ”Rewrite vs. Truncate” Divergence.</span> Comparing answer lengths before (Blue) and after (Orange) defense. While most models achieve safety by aggressively truncating responses (significant negative <math id="S5.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>), GPT-5 uniquely <span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">increases</span> answer length (<math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+110" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>110</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+110</annotation></semantics></math> chars), demonstrating its capacity to generate nuanced, safe explanations rather than refusing.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21496v1/qualified_answer_stats.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Comparison of the percentage of ”qualified responses” (explicit admissions of insufficient information) across models. While baseline models (Blue) exhibit overconfidence, answering nearly all queries, including those based on false premises, <span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> (Orange) induces a dramatic behavioral shift. By empowering models to admit ”insufficient information,” the defense effectively neutralizes the risk of generating plausible but false semantic content (hallucinations).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Scale-Dependent Safety Behaviors</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A critical question in agentic alignment is whether safety behaviors scale predictably with model size (RQ3). Our analysis of answer length and convergence rates reveals a fundamental divergence: advanced reasoning models achieve safety through <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">transformation</span>, while capacity-constrained models achieve safety through <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">elimination</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The ”Rewrite vs Truncate” Divergence.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 The Privacy-Utility Frontier ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates the change in answer length (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta L</annotation></semantics></math>) following the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> intervention, revealing two distinct behavioral clusters. We observe constructive safety in GPT-5, which uniquely exhibits a positive length change (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+110" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>110</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+110</annotation></semantics></math> characters). Upon detecting semantic leakage, the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Editor</span> agent does not delete the sensitive span; instead, it <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rewrites and expands</span> the context to explain nuances or provide non-sensitive alternatives. This explains why GPT-5 retains high utility (8.58) despite aggressive privacy filtering. Conversely, smaller and mid-sized models display <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Destructive Safety</span>, exhibiting massive length reductions ranging from <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-50\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-73\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>73</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-73\%</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Llama-3.3-70B drops from 1479 to 394 characters). Lacking the reasoning capacity to execute subtle semantic edits, these models default to the ”safest” heuristic: removing the content entirely. While effective for privacy (occurrence drops to 19.0%), this strategy imposes a severe utility tax.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence Efficiency.</span>
The ablation study in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.5 Robustness &amp; Validation ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 The Privacy-Utility Frontier ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> further highlights this ”Reasoning Gap.” Advanced models demonstrate fast convergence, with GPT-5 frequently satisfying the <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Evaluator</span> constraints at <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> or even during the Initialization phase (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>), requiring minimal corrective loops. In contrast, smaller models (e.g., Gemma-3-4B) encounter a capacity wall and struggle to converge. Despite exhausting the maximum iteration budget (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=3</annotation></semantics></math>), they often fail to resolve the semantic leakage (staying at <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>90% occurrence). This suggests that below a certain reasoning threshold, agentic feedback loops yield diminishing returns, as the model cannot effectively critique or repair its own semantic errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These findings indicate that <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic Refinement is not model-agnostic</span>. The ”Editor” role requires a minimum threshold of reasoning capability; below this threshold, the loop devolves into a simple refusal mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>The Double-Edged Sword of Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the emergence of reasoning-enhanced models (e.g., OpenAI o1, Qwen-QwQ), a critical question arises: <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does reasoning aid or hinder privacy?</span> We investigate this by toggling the ”Thinking Mode” (Chain-of-Thought) on Qwen-3-8B. The results, presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 The Double-Edged Sword of Reasoning ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, reveal a distinct <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy Paradox</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the absence of defense (<span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No Protection</span>), enabling reasoning capabilities significantly <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">increases</span> semantic leakage (Occurrence rises from 74.0% to <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">84.0%</span>). We hypothesize that reasoning chains allow the model to more effectively ”connect the dots” between benign public facts to infer sensitive attributes—a phenomenon known as the <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mosaic Effect</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pozen, 2005</a>)</cite>. In this unconstrained setting, higher intelligence correlates with higher risk, as the model uses its compute to hallucinate detailed (and sensitive) narratives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, when <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> is active, reasoning capabilities become a powerful asset. The reasoning-enabled defense outperforms the standard defense in both safety (42.0% vs. 47.0% leakage) and utility (4.61 vs. 4.03 score).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthesis.</span>
This suggests that reasoning capabilities act as a <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">magnitude multiplier</span> for the system prompt. When the objective is purely ”helpfulness” (Baseline), reasoning amplifies leakage. When the objective includes ”safety constraints” (Feedback), <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> utilizes the reasoning budget to solve the complex constraint satisfaction problem, finding a rewrite that preserves semantic value while excising sensitive inferences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S5.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of ”Thinking Mode” on Privacy and Utility (Qwen-3-8B).</span> We observe a <span id="S5.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Privacy Paradox</span>: Reasoning capabilities <span id="S5.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">increase</span> leakage in the baseline (84% vs 74%) by enabling stronger inferences, yet they <span id="S5.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">enhance</span> the effectiveness of <span id="S5.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> (42% vs 47%), allowing the model to navigate the privacy-utility trade-off more successfully (Utility 4.61 vs 4.03).</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.12" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:69.7pt;vertical-align:-32.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-10.1pt,2.1pt) scale(0.941938533507641,0.941938533507641) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.12.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
