</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Manual Evaluation of Contextual Utility in Qualified Responses.</span> We conducted a qualitative audit of 10 <span id="S5.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">qualified responses</span> (defined as explicit admissions of insufficient information) per model. The goal was to determine if the model, despite admitting ignorance, provided useful context (e.g., citing safety standards or clarifying misconceptions) rather than a simple refusal. The results indicate a strong correlation between model scale and reasoning capability: <span id="S5.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">larger models</span> (GPT-5, Qwen3-235B) successfully preserved utility in 80% of these qualified responses, whereas smaller models frequently failed to restructure the narrative effectively.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:50.7pt;vertical-align:-23.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-34.1pt,5.3pt) scale(0.827842991855831,0.827842991855831) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
