</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Epistemic Uncertainty &amp; Hallucination</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key hypothesis of this work is that semantic leakage can be a symptom of hallucination, where models invent plausible but unverified details to satisfy a prompt. We investigate whether <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> can restore <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">epistemic calibration</span> (RQ4).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shift to Qualified Responses.</span> We analyzed the frequency of ”qualified responses,” defined as explicit admissions of insufficient information (e.g., ”I cannot verify this claim”). As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 The Privacy-Utility Frontier ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, baseline models exhibit dangerous overconfidence, answering nearly 100% of queries regardless of factual basis. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> induces a dramatic shift in behavior, causing an increased rate of qualified responses (e.g., rising from 0% to 70.5% for Llama-3.3).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Context Preservation vs. Simple Refusal.</span> Crucially, this shift toward epistemic humility does not necessarily imply a loss of utility. As detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 The Double-Edged Sword of Reasoning ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, our manual audit reveals a distinct scaling law regarding the quality of these refusals. <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Larger models</span> (GPT-5, Qwen3-235B) demonstrated the reasoning capacity to successfully provide useful context, such as citing relevant safety standards or clarifying misconceptions, in <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">80%</span> of qualified responses. In contrast, smaller models (e.g., Qwen3-8B) struggled with this nuance, frequently defaulting to simple refusals with low context preservation (30%). This suggests that while <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> effectively stops hallucination across the board, the ability to <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">restructure</span> the qualified responses into useful context is an emergent property of model scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduction in False Information.</span> Finally, our manual audit of 88 qualified responses generated by GPT-5 confirms the safety benefits of this approach. While <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">15.9%</span> of baseline queries resulted in the generation of false information, the false information rate under <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> dropped to <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0%</span>, confirming that the method effectively restores epistemic calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Robustness &amp; Validation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common critique of LLM-based evaluation is <span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Circular Evaluation Bias</span>, the risk that a model might preferentially rate its own outputs or that safety classifiers are overfitted to specific architectures. We validate the robustness of our results through two rigorous checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment with Human Judgment.</span>
Prior work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite> established that GPT-4-class models achieve high alignment with human annotators for semantic privacy tasks (<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math> correlation). Building on this, we utilize GPT-5 as our primary judge due to its reasoning depth. To validate this automated evaluation, we conducted a manual audit of GPT-5’s judgments on a stratified sample of 400 responses (split between <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No Protection</span> and <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>). As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.5 Robustness &amp; Validation ‣ 5 Empirical Results &amp; Analysis ‣ Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we observed consistently high agreement between human annotators and GPT-5, particularly for Privacy (<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\geq 96.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>96.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\geq 96.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and Misinformation (<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\geq 91.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>91.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\geq 91.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>). The high overall agreement (<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>) confirms that GPT-5 serves as a reliable and rigorous proxy for human judgment in detecting semantic leakage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="S5.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human agreement with GPT-5 evaluations.</span> We conducted manual labeling of GPT-5 evaluations of 200 GPT-5 responses in No Protection and <span id="S5.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>. <math id="S5.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the rate of agreement with <span id="S5.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">privacy</span> evaluations; <math id="S5.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the rate of agreement with the evaluation of <span id="S5.T4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harmful</span> contents; <math id="S5.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the rate of agreement with evaluations of <span id="S5.T4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">misinformation</span>. <math id="S5.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S5.T4.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overall agreement</span> with a particular evaluation as a whole and is defined as <math id="S5.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\ \wedge\ \mathcal{H}\ \wedge\ \mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.722em" rspace="0.722em">∧</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0.722em" rspace="0.722em">∧</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\ \wedge\ \mathcal{H}\ \wedge\ \mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T4.14" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T4.14.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.14.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S5.T4.14.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T4.14.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T4.14.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T4.14.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.14.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.14.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">No Protection</th>
<td id="S5.T4.14.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">96.97%</td>
