<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">   Iteration <math id="S5.T5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=3</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.6.1.17.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">90.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.6.1.17.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.96</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">32.32</td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.5" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb"></td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3.65</td>
<td id="S5.T5.6.1.17.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1247</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work investigated the limits of Large Language Models in self-regulating Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI). By shifting the defense paradigm from <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static refusal</span> to <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">agentic rewriting</span>, we demonstrate that the ”Privacy-Utility Trade-off” is not an immutable law, but a function of model reasoning capability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold. First, we establish <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> as a robust inference-time framework that navigates the Pareto frontier, reducing semantic leakage by <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.6%</span> while incurring only a marginal <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9.8%</span> utility cost. Second, we uncover a fundamental <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scale-Dependent Divergence</span> in safety behaviors: while large reasoning models (e.g., GPT-5) utilize feedback to synthesize nuanced, safe narratives, smaller models revert to aggressive truncation that destroys utility. Third, we identify a critical link between privacy and truthfulness, showing that agentic defenses effectively convert ”confident hallucinations” about false premises into ”principled refusals.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations &amp; Future Work.</span>
The primary cost of agentic defense is inference latency; our iterative loop increases compute requirements by <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> compared to a standard forward pass. While acceptable for high-stakes applications, real-time deployment requires optimization.
Ultimately, our findings suggest that privacy is not merely a constraint to be imposed, but a reasoning task to be solved. As models advance in reasoning capabilities, their ability to protect user privacy without compromising helpfulness will likely emerge as a distinct measure of intelligence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgment</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Umid Suleymanov and Murat Kantarcioglu were supported in part by The Commonwealth cyber initiative grants and by National Science Foundation under Grant ITE-2452833.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The authors also thank ADA University’s Center for Data Analytics Research (CeDAR) for providing GPU computing resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a framework for mitigating Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI) leakage in Large Language Models. Current safety paradigms often rely on ”refusal-heavy” alignment, which disproportionately degrades model utility for complex or sensitive topics. by demonstrating that safety can be achieved through <span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rewriting</span> rather than <span id="Sx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">refusal</span>, our work suggests a path toward alignment techniques that respect user intent while preserving privacy. This is particularly relevant for deploying LLMs in high-stakes domains (e.g., healthcare, finance) where ”I cannot answer” is often an unacceptable failure mode. Furthermore, our finding that agentic oversight reduces hallucinations suggests that privacy defenses can simultaneously serve as epistemic guardrails, reducing the propagation of incorrect information.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This paper contains examples of generated text that may be considered harmful, offensive, or containing misinformation, including sensitive identity attributes and reputation-harming viewpoints. These examples are included solely for the purpose of scientific analysis and to demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed <span id="Sx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span> defense. They do not represent the views of the authors or their affiliations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Open Science</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate reproducibility and encourage further research into semantic privacy, we have released our code, datasets, and experimental scripts. The complete implementation of <span id="Sx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SemSIEdit</span>, along with the model outputs and evaluation protocols, is available at: <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/SemSIEdit-3231" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/SemSIEdit-3231</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
